WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.439
So what we're going to try to talk about

00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:04.080
today is the arithmetic of dynamical

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.150
sequences. Let's do an example; so here's

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.519
an example maybe you'll recognize. Let's

00:00:08.519 --> 00:00:12.269
let f of x be the function 2x plus 1, and

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:14.940
what I mean by iterating something under

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.770
this function is let's start at a point

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.680
say x equals 0. When we plug x equals 0

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.199
into this function we get 1 and then

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:24.060
let's keep doing that; so when we plug 1

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.310
into the function we get 2 plus 1 which

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.070
is 3, when we plug 3 into the function we

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.890
get 6 plus 1 which is 7. So you look at

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.670
the last number - so 15, 31. So maybe I'll

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.260
do two more just for a demonstration

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:43.410
here, so 62 plus 1 that's 63 and 126 plus

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:47.460
1 it's 127. So this sequence is special,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.440
so the sequence- you might recognize that

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.210
each of these numbers in the sequence

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.140
are off from a power of 2 by exactly 1.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.090
so this is known as the Mersenne

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.160
sequence. Well people have heard of the

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.960
Mersenne sequence because there's a

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.730
famous question that we don't know how

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.189
to answer is are there infinitely many

00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:07.860
prime numbers in this sequence? Since

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.900
this question is so hard, one easier

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.479
question we could ask would be what are

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:14.520
the prime divisors of each element of

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.320
this sequence look like? 
- (Brady: That seems like)

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.869
(a harder question!)
- Well it might be a

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:20.040
harder question depending on how

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.229
specific you want to get. But so what I

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:24.030
mean by the prime divisors of elements

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.920
of the sequence is we have these factors,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.140
so each number factors into prime

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.240
numbers as long as we're ignoring 0 here.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.590
So 3 is already prime, 7 is already prime,

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:38.210
but 15 this is 3 times 5.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:44.250
31 that's already prime, 63 well let's

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.729
see - so 7 is a prime divisor and 9 is

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:49.290
a divisor but it's not prime, it's

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:55.290
actually 3 times 3 times 7.
And 127 - let's

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.780
see I think that's prime; and so on.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.549
Anyway so this is what I mean by prime

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:03.000
divisors. And so instead of asking, okay,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.310
do we have infinitely many elements in

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.409
this sequence which are prime? We could

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:09.899
at least ask as we go along in the

00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:11.610
sequence do we get a new prime at each

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.830
step? So that's the
question I'm interested

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.350
in answering. So here, okay we don't have

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.270
any prime devisers, but here 3 is a

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.630
prime divisor that never appeared before,

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.490
here 7 is a prime divisor that never

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:27.440
appeared before, here 5 never appeared,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.990
31 never appeared - but here we run into a

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:31.710
problem.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.890
(We broke the streak.)
- No new primes.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.500
(And is that bad?)
- Well depends on what you're

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.770
interested in. But if the question is, do

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.050
we always have a new prime divisor? For

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.640
the Mersenne sequence the answer is no

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.260
because look at the sixth element -

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.070
doesn't have a new prime divisor. But the

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:54.470
interesting fact is after that all

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.690
Mersenne numbers have a new prime divisor.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.270
(Every single one?)
- Every single one.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:06.450
(What happened with 63?)
- Well it's just- when

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.920
you're at the beginning of a sequence

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.470
the numbers are so close and there's so

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.440
many primes happening that it can occur

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.060
that you sort of fail to have this new

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.370
prime divisor. And it also matters that

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.810
this was the sixth element of the

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:19.980
sequence, but that's a little bit

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.769
complicated. So the point is that after

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.570
that after that sixth element of the

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.060
sequence, after 63, we always have the new

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:28.830
prime divisors. So this is an interesting

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.660
thing and I think the next natural

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.670
question once you notice that a sequence

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.739
like this has some interesting property

00:03:34.739 --> 00:03:37.440
is what's special about this sequence?

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.780
What other sequences have this property?

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.140
So instead of choosing a function like

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.959
2x plus 1 to generate my sequence what

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.959
if I chose a nonlinear function? So if I

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:51.540
had an x squared in there. So let's say I

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.959
chose x squared plus 1. But we can still

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:57.570
start generating these sequences; so if

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.820
we start with 0, if we plug it into this

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.910
function, it is going to 
map to 1 again but

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.040
when we plug 1 in we get 1 plus 1

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.890
which is 2. When we plug 2 in we have 2

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.250
squared plus 1 which is 5. We plug 5 in

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.810
we have 25 plus 1 which is 26; and when

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.450
we plug 26 in I happen to know we end up

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.740
with 677. So because I'm dealing with an

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.990
x squared instead of an x here the

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.020
sequence gets larger 
much faster but still

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.270
we get this sequence of integers, still we

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.280
can ask when do we start getting new

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.729
prime divisors? So if we look at what we

00:04:35.729 --> 00:04:38.069
have here- so notice again 2 &amp; 5 are

00:04:38.069 --> 00:04:44.430
already prime; 26 is 2 times 13 and 677

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:48.360
is also prime. And it turns out that for

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.580
this sequence it's even better than

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.090
Mersenne sequence: all numbers after 2

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.580
have a new prime divisor. So this has

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.370
proven that after 2 you always get a new

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:00.960
prime divisor in this particular

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.759
sequence.
- (You showed me an amazing)

00:05:02.759 --> 00:05:03.870
(sequence; then you showed me a more)

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.259
(amazing sequence!)
- Ok well then get ready!

00:05:06.259 --> 00:05:09.750
So let's do the same thing, except that

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.819
there's no reason that we're only

00:05:11.819 --> 00:05:14.039
talking about positive integers here,

00:05:14.039 --> 00:05:17.880
right? So let's look instead at something

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.940
like this and see what happens. So again

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.940
we start with 0; 0 maps to negative 1,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.120
negative 1 maps to 1 squared minus 1

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.900
which is 0. But we already know where 0

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.409
maps - 0 maps to negative 1 and so the

00:05:34.409 --> 00:05:37.772
sequence we get is completely not interesting.

00:05:37.772 --> 00:05:41.160
- (I think that's cool! How can 
you say that's not interesting?)

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.180
Well it's definitely not

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.800
going to have this property that we have

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.889
new prime divisors at least. So this is

00:05:46.889 --> 00:05:49.529
bad news, right? As far as this question

00:05:49.529 --> 00:05:52.440
is concerned this is a weird sequence. So

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.539
let's let's try a different negative

00:05:54.539 --> 00:05:58.740
number, let's try- I don't want to try

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.630
minus 2 and maybe you can try it and

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.990
find out why, let's try instead minus 3.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:07.680
All right so let's go; so 0, and
0 maps to 0

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.990
minus 3, minus 3 maps to 9 minus 3 which

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:19.800
is 6, 6 maps to 36 
minus 3 - 33, 33 maps to

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.360
something messy that I don't want to

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.060
calculate. But now we'll get an infinite

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.949
sequence again; instead of having this

00:06:25.949 --> 00:06:28.259
this repetition like we had in the minus

00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:30.599
1 case we'll get an infinite sequence

00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:34.650
and again this sequence will have new

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:37.229
prime divisors at each
point in the sequence.

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:39.990
Every time.
- (I'm beginning to think this isn't)

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:45.600
(such a special thing anymore.)
- I know; so let

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.660
me tell you you're sort of right. So here

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.300
I'll write down a general fact: if say c

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.819
is an integer - so a whole number or the

00:06:53.819 --> 00:06:56.330
negative of a whole number - and c is not

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:00.690
0 minus 1 or minus 2, mostly because we

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.210
want to avoid this problem, then every

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:06.750
element of the sequence that's generated

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:09.389
from x squared plus c has a new prime

00:07:09.389 --> 00:07:12.389
divisor.
- (So the only exceptions are 0,)

00:07:12.389 --> 00:07:15.419
(minus 1, minus 2?)
- This works for x

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:17.880
squareds but it works for x squareds

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.280
plus an integer. If you wanted you could

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:22.620
ask this for a fraction c instead of a

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.889
whole number c. So if c was 1/2 let's

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:28.919
say, so if f of x is x squared plus 1/2

00:07:28.919 --> 00:07:31.050
well then what starts happening? So 0

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.259
maps to 1/2; now the computation is a

00:07:34.259 --> 00:07:37.470
little less pleasant but 1/2 is 1/4 plus

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:41.250
1/2 which is 3/4. 3/4 when we put it into

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:45.090
this function we have 9 over 16 plus 1/2

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.900
which I guess is 17 over 16? Does that

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.080
sound right? And so on. So even though

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.430
we're getting these fractions in the

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:55.560
sequence the denominators are just

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.990
powers of 2. And so when you try and

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.030
factor them the only prime that you get

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.190
there is 2 so we can still ask the same

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.259
question about the numerators in the

00:08:04.259 --> 00:08:05.400
sequence.
- (So we're just going to- we're)

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.110
(just going to ignore the denominators?)
- Just ignore

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.820
the denominators, there's not interesting

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.520
stuff there happening there anyways. So

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.099
if you ignore the denominators then we

00:08:14.099 --> 00:08:18.690
look and we have this sequence 0, 1, 3, 17

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:20.880
and so on; and it turns out that again

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.250
you will have this property. It seems

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:25.710
sort of fundamental and part of the

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:27.539
reason why is because I've been hiding

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:31.860
an example from you. So- so maybe one last

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:33.810
example of computation that I'll show

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.820
you is this kind of tricky little thing

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.810
here: x squared minus 7 over 4.
So what does

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:44.099
0 map to? 0 maps to minus 7 over 4, 21

00:08:44.099 --> 00:08:49.079
over 16, minus 7 over 16 squared. All of a

00:08:49.079 --> 00:08:50.459
sudden everything comes to a stop

00:08:50.459 --> 00:08:53.760
because look at our numerators - 0,

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.370
7, 7 times 3 - so 3 is a

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:59.040
new prime divisor there - but here we

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.730
don't have a new prime divisor. So

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:04.590
there's something different between 1/2

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:08.460
and minus 7/4; for some reason there's an

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:09.870
element in this sequence where the

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:11.880
numerator doesn't have a new prime

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.100
divisor.
- (And this is- is this the only- this)

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.290
(is the only number, the only fraction)

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:17.364
(that throws the spanner in the works?)

00:09:17.364 --> 00:09:20.820
We don't know, we don't know.
So just to give

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.660
you a hint of why this is true: so this

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.910
is the only one that we know of, but we

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:28.470
don't know if it's the only time it can

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:30.840
possibly happen. So what's really going

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.730
on behind the scenes here has something

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:36.470
to do with a particular set of numbers

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:39.240
known as the Mandelbrot set which I

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.430
won't go into but it's this interesting

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:44.070
fractal where things go kind of crazy,

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:47.460
and it's hard to draw a fractal so I'll

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.400
just do my best. 
- (Not a bad effort.)

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.350
Thank you! So let me

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:54.480
put this into perspective for you, so

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.280
this is a picture of the complex numbers

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:59.130
and I'm interested in the numbers that

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:03.240
are inside of this set. And let's look at

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.740
the values of c that we've done this

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:08.220
computation for: so 1, we first did x

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.950
squared plus 1, 1 is over here. We

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:13.770
also did minus 1, minus 1 is over

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:16.890
here. We did minus 3 which is well

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:18.960
outside of this set. We did one half

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.570
which is also not inside this set. But

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.060
minus 7 over 4, it is inside of

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.190
this set. So minus 7 over 4 it

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:28.980
turns out is right inside this piece of

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:33.120
the Mandelbrot set. 
- (This line of the real)

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.740
(of the real numbers there)

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:38.220
(seems to run right through 
a whole swath of)

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.890
(fractions that would be- is that- aren't)

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:42.180
(there going to be loads of fractions)

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:43.810
(that are going to cause
this problem then?)

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:45.000
That's a really good point. So

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:46.560
you're absolutely right, there's there's

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:48.330
tons and tons of fractions on the real

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.580
line inside of the Mandelbrot set, but

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.380
it's not enough it turns out to just be

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:54.450
inside the Mandelbrot set you have to be

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.850
particularly very far inside of the

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.800
Mandelbrot set. And so this is really

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.140
saying something special about where the

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.120
number c can lie inside of the

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.640
Mandelbrot set. 
- (So if there are other)

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:06.540
(numbers like)

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.730
(minus 7 over 4 that are going to)

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:11.010
(throw a spanner in our works they)

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.900
(definitely have to be in here?)
- That's

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:15.120
right. So right away you get for free

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.700
that if you take a large enough fraction,

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.770
say bigger than 1 for example although

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:22.140
you could get closer, for free you get

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:24.090
that there will always be new primes in

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:26.640
those sequences. But yeah, if we were to

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:28.260
look for other examples of where this

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.360
might occur they would have to lie

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.100
inside of the Mandelbrot set and they

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:33.570
would have to lie well inside the

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:35.540
Mandelbrot set actually it turns out.

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:37.080
(But it looks like there are 
numbers here)

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:38.520
(that would be pretty well inside the)

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:39.650
(Mandelbrot set, or is it
a particular like-)

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:42.570
It's a particular thing. Inside each of

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.550
these bulbs here that I've drawn sort of

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:47.400
half half heartedly there's a special

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:50.790
point. And what might happen, the only

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:52.410
reason why this might happen, is if your

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:55.080
rational number is very very close to

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.420
that special point in a technical way

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:00.780
that is is hard to formulate. I think at

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:02.280
heart people don't believe that there's

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:05.280
one beyond minus 7 over 4. If I had

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:07.550
to make a guess I would say probably not,

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:09.690
but it would certainly be nice to know

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.080
that for sure.

